.TH KEEPWSL.SERVICE 8 "%DATE%" "%NAME% %VERSION%"
.SH NAME
keepwsl.service \- keep WSL alive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keepwsl.service
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) has a feature where it automatically
shuts down if there are no console windows in the foreground and there are
no background processes that are not managed by systemd. While this often
can help in conserving energy and system resources, it gets in the way of
running graphical applications and desktop environments on WSL.
\fIkeepwsl.service\fP circumvents this feature by using the \fIwsl\fP(1) wrapper
script to create a low-intensity, perpetual background process whenever a
graphical session is active. This means that, if \fIkeepwsl.service\fP is
enabled, it activates automatically whenever the systemd graphical target
is active.
.PP
If you have installed @NAME@ from your package manager, \fIkeepwsl.service\fP
will not be enabled by default. If you wish to use desktop environments on
WSL, you may enable it using 'systemctl \fBenable\fP keepwsl.service'.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fIwsl\fP(1), \fIwsl\-tools\-services\fP(8)
